// Generated by npm run build:docs. Admin-only API; not under public/.
export const RELEASE_DOCS=[{"name":"V15-0-0-FEDERATED-LIBRARY.md","content":"# WASP v15.0.0 — Federated library, storage audit, and admin improvements\n\n## Release status\n\n**Staging candidate.** The code and automated tests were run locally. Docker image construction, production Cloudflare D1/R2, AWS/Tailscale Bridge, cross-server streaming, and a live production upgrade have **not** been exercised in this environment. Back up and stage before deploying. No production data was changed to prepare this release.\n\n## Upgrade first: repair broken v14.0.3 migrations\n\nv14.0.3 could mark a schema as `14.3` while failing to create `peer_catalogue_chunks` because multiple SQL strings were passed into a single-argument `DB.prepare()` helper. The v15 upgrade checks that earlier marker and independently creates missing tables, including `peer_catalogue_chunks`, `peer_catalogue_cache`, `recent_listens`, and `file_fingerprints`. Existing track records, user accounts, playlists, R2 objects, scanner volumes, and admin settings are not reset. The schema marker advances to `15` after required statements succeed. The old v14 D1 parameter-limit optimization remains in place.\n\n## Primary peer as local\n\nUnder **Admin → Network → Connected peers**, one peer may be selected using a radio button as **Treat as local**; choose **None** to disable it. Only one peer is selected at a time; removing that peer also clears the selection. The selected peer's playable tracks, server playlists, public user playlists, and artists are shown under the local browsing categories and **In your library** labels rather than Peer Library. Tracks retain their true origin and are streamed from the source, without downloading audio. Playlist IDs remain namespaced to avoid collisions.\n\n**Network limitation:** a Cloudflare Worker cannot directly fetch a private Tailscale address. To make a private Docker server the public Cloudflare website's primary source, expose a suitably secured and reachable endpoint (such as a properly configured Cloudflare Tunnel/reverse proxy) and validate artwork, audio, account/privacy, CORS and playlist behavior. This release does **not** implement that secure outbound publishing/tunnel, automatic R2 migration, or R2 removal. Metadata-only snapshots are available where the current peer-synchronization workflow succeeds.\n\n## SHA-256 and duplicate management\n\n**Admin → Duplicate management** displays progress, exact-byte duplicate counts, source paths and safe deletion controls. The scanner works on Docker only, reads the configured `/music` folder, hashes audio as a stream, and sends only fingerprints and metadata to the local Wrangler/D1 database. The existing `/music:ro` mount remains read-only. The hashing operation does not rewrite tags, modify filenames, move or delete audio. Previously fingerprinted, unchanged files are skipped using size and modification time. Enable a schedule in **Network → Crons → SHA-256 hash local /music folder** (off by default) or request a scan manually. Hashing a large library uses disk read bandwidth: plan it for a quiet period.\n\nUploads compare their supplied SHA-256 fingerprint against uploaded files **and** already-indexed folder fingerprints. Exact duplicate groups may include multiple different paths. Different encodings, modified tags, or different MP3 bytes do not have the same SHA-256; acoustic fingerprinting is not included.\n\n**Deletion limitations and safeguards:** the administrator must explicitly review and confirm. The app never deletes a file in `/music`. Only an uploaded object with an identical surviving fingerprint can be deleted, and only if it has no playlist references, reactions, or plays. Folder scan evidence must be recent to justify deleting a duplicate against an original in `/music`. Existing records are not automatically deduplicated or rewritten; other user data remains intact. **Back up first.** You can keep all duplicates if desired.\n\n## Permanent Docker HTTPS configuration\n\nThe deployed Docker image uses `docker/Caddyfile`; both that file and the root `Caddyfile` now explicitly configure `https://{$SITE_ADDRESS:localhost}` and proxy the Docker gateway on `127.0.0.1:8788`. Set `SITE_ADDRESS` to the actual IP/hostname used to connect, **without a protocol or port**, and set `DEFAULT_SNI` to match in Docker Compose/Portainer. HTTP on 8080 and HTTPS on 8443 remain in one container. The corrected image persists across reboots and redeployments without a host-specific bind mount. A rebuilt image is required to pick up this repository change.\n\n`TLS_ARGS=internal` (the existing default) uses Caddy's private certificate authority. This encrypts traffic but is not automatically trusted by browsers; use an appropriate certificate trust setup or a public hostname with a correctly configured publicly trusted certificate for public production deployments. Merely clicking through a browser warning is not the same as a trusted HTTPS environment. The upload UI now displays a clear message when `crypto.subtle` is unavailable due to HTTP or untrusted context.\n\n## Recently listened\n\nThe newest 50 confirmed listening events remain in reverse chronological order and update approximately every 10 seconds using local and reachable peer feeds. The UI now shows artwork, title, artist, and play count, without displaying date or time. Private listeners' usernames and IP addresses are not published.\n\n## Branding and sidebar credits\n\nUnder **Admin → General settings → Branding**, switch the top-left site name between text and a custom raster image. Branding image, browser tab name, and favicon are independent. A new **Sidebar credits and branding** section below Branding and above Advertising accepts optional HTML/CSS/JavaScript for credits, links, images, badges, or powered-by notices. It is blank and disabled by default. Custom markup executes in a sandboxed iframe without same-origin permission or access to application authentication; public settings can be cached by browsers.\n\n## Update documentations\n\nAn admin-only **Update documentations** navigation item is below the sidebar separator, immediately above View site. It presents release notes from `docs/*.md` with GitHub-like Markdown formatting, tables, headings, links and code blocks. Sources are bundled into `src/release-docs.js` using `npm run build:docs` rather than being published as public static Markdown files. Only authenticated admins can retrieve documents through `/api/admin/docs`. This is a read-only viewer.\n\n## Deployment / acceptance checklist\n\n1. Back up Cloudflare production D1/R2 and the Docker local D1 state, `/data/uploads`, `/music` source, Caddy state and settings/Portainer stack definition. Record current version and container/image digest. Do not clean a database or delete volumes.\n2. Stage the **same v15 source** on a nonproduction Docker stack or Worker and a copied database first. Confirm that a v14.0.3 DB with marker `14.3` but no `peer_catalogue_chunks` table repairs itself and retains playlists/users.\n3. Check `/api/peer`, `/api/tracks`, `/api/peer-catalogue`, `/api/playlists`, and the new **Duplicate management** admin page. Try sync and refresh. Confirm the radio primary selection changes the local playlist/artist labels and that the original audio streams from the peer.\n4. Configure `SITE_ADDRESS`, `DEFAULT_SNI`, and certificate trust. Verify TLS and MP3 upload on a trusted HTTPS browser connection. **No special AWS UDP port is necessary for the HTTPS TCP service.**\n5. Keep the SHA-256 cron disabled initially, scan a few controlled files manually, review unchanged contents/hashes and database progress, then enable a suitable schedule. Verify the duplicate counter and confirm that any file with playlist references is refused deletion.\n6. Test branding, the isolated sidebar iframe, admin documentation permissions, user access, listening activity, playlist covers, copied playlists, and Most listened before migrating production traffic.\n7. Roll back the **application image only** if needed. The additive schema tables may remain in place; do not roll back by restoring/deleting the entire live database unless a deliberate tested recovery plan requires it.\n\n## Automated verification performed\n\nBackend API tests and browser regressions run against in-memory SQLite mocks and Chromium. They do not simulate Cloudflare networking, full Docker image build, cloud certificate issuance, Portainer, or production loads. Streaming and live cross-peer behavior still need staging verification.\n"},{"name":"V14-D1-OPTIMIZATION.md","content":"# WASP v14 — Cloudflare D1 Optimization\n\nThis release focuses on the main Cloudflare-hosted WASP deployment (`music.playy.online`). It is based on the complete WASP v13 Artist & Playlist Reactions release. **Bridge/Nginx caching is intentionally deferred.**\n\n## Why this release exists\n\nProduction Cloudflare D1 Query Insights showed approximately 6 million rows read in a 24-hour view. Four queries resembling `SELECT artist, artists FROM tracks WHERE instr(...) ...` accounted for about 5.22 million of those reads. An `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` run on the live D1 database confirmed `SCAN tracks`; the database had indexes for the primary key and track hash, but not artist or album lookups.\n\nThese are *observations from the supplied dashboard*, not a measured before-and-after benchmark. We cannot promise a particular reduction until a staging and production comparison has been performed.\n\n## Changes in v14\n\n### 1. Indexed artist membership\n\n- Adds a non-destructive `track_artist_map` table, with `(track_id, artist_key)` primary key and `idx_track_artist_lookup(artist_key, album)` index.\n- Populates it once from the existing `tracks` metadata, using the same multi-artist parsing already used throughout WASP. This includes collaborations and protected band names such as `AC/DC`.\n- Replaces repeated `instr(artist, ?)` / `instr(artists, ?)` whole-library scans in `libraryHasArtist()` with indexed lookups.\n- Keeps the map in sync on web uploads, local-folder scanning, track edits, deletion, and full-library cleanups. Original `tracks` records and audio objects are preserved during migration.\n- Adds an index on `tracks(album)` and ordering indexes for playlist-track tables.\n\n**First migration:** The new mapping and indexes require a one-time read of the existing catalogue and some D1 writes. WASP records `wasp_schema_version = 14` so normal requests and future Worker instances no longer rerun v13's CREATE/ALTER statements. A D1-backed short-lived migration lease prevents simultaneous backfills by many Worker isolates; while it runs, some requests might briefly need to retry. The migration is not a music transfer or an R2 rewrite.\n\n### 2. Regional Cloudflare public response cache\n\nOnly these exact, public, session-independent **GET** endpoints are cached by the Worker Cache API:\n\n| Route | Edge TTL |\n|---|---:|\n| `/api/tracks` | 90 seconds |\n| `/api/playlists` | 120 seconds |\n| `/api/user-playlists` (public only) | 60 seconds |\n| `/api/site` | 120 seconds |\n| `/api/entity-reactions` (public aggregates) | 5 seconds |\n| `/api/track-reaction-counts` (new public aggregates) | 5 seconds |\n\nThe cache is checked *before* D1 initialization or the sharing-setting query. A warm cache hit can therefore serve the JSON without any D1 request. Cache entries use a versioned key, and selected admin mutations invalidate affected entries on a best-effort basis. Entries also expire after their TTL; updates may take up to the TTL to become visible in another data center.\n\n**Never shared-cached:** authentication, private playlists, accounts, passwords, admin requests, session-specific reaction-state, request queues, personal histories, and any write requests. The cache is regional and Cloudflare may evict it at any time. These caches **reduce D1 queries on hits but do not bypass Worker invocation**; separate Workers Caching is not enabled because this application has routes requiring strict private authorization.\n\nInspect the `x-wasp-cache` response header on a public route (`MISS` or `HIT`) when the Cache API is available; cache hits are not guaranteed when consecutive requests land at different data centers.\n\n### 3. Smaller periodic reaction refresh\n\nWhen a visitor is viewing reaction charts, the browser now loads `/api/track-reaction-counts` rather than re-downloading the full `/api/tracks` music catalogue every 15 seconds. Personal vote state still comes from `/api/reaction-state` and `/api/peer-reactions`, and immediate optimistic voting remains as in v13.\n\n### 4. Safe rollout and compatibility\n\nThe new schema is additive. The only new table is `track_artist_map`, plus new indexes and the schema-version/lease settings rows. Existing tracks, R2 music, users, playlist contents, reactions, peer metadata, Docker volumes and settings remain intact. Rolling back to v13 code leaves the new table unused; it is not necessary to delete it.\n\n## Recommended staged rollout\n\n1. **Back up production first.** Use the Cloudflare D1 Time Travel bookmark/export or another tested backup method. Do not clear your D1 database or R2 bucket.\n2. Deploy the v14 ZIP into a **separate staging Worker with separate D1 and R2 bindings**. Avoid pushing untested v14 code straight to the Git-connected production branch.\n3. Populate staging with a representative catalogue (and playlist/reaction data where permitted). Run the bundled tests using Node 22+ and optional Playwright.\n4. After deployment, the first request creates the indexes and artist map. Verify `PRAGMA index_list('track_artist_map');` and `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN SELECT 1 FROM track_artist_map WHERE artist_key = 'ac dc' LIMIT 1;` (should indicate indexed SEARCH rather than `SCAN tracks`).\n5. Verify searching and opening local and peer artists, biographies, similar artists, playlist playback, and reactions. Check `GET /api/tracks` response for `x-wasp-cache`; validate repeated reads and cache invalidation on admin edits.\n6. Deploy on production **after the daily D1 quota reset**, ideally at a low-traffic time, only after staging succeeds. Watch Worker error rates and D1 Metrics / Query Insights. A one-time migration can incur extra rows read/written at deploy time.\n7. Compare rows read per 24 hours or per 1,000 HTTP visits across similar traffic windows. Focus on the disappearance of repeated `instr()` artist queries in Query Insights and measure actual cache hit behavior.\n\n## Run local tests\n\n```bash\nnode tests/v14_d1_optimization.test.mjs\nnode tests/api_smoke.mjs\nnode tests/v13_entity_reaction_playlists.test.mjs\nnode tests/gateway_proxy.test.mjs\n# Optional with Python Playwright + Chromium:\npython tests/ui_smoke.py\n```\n\n## Known limits / not included\n\n- Full live AWS EC2 Docker image and live Cloudflare streaming were not tested in this packaging environment.\n- A D1 Cache API hit is specific to the data center handling the request. Cold caches and new unique requests still execute SQL.\n- Newly added track/album/playlist metadata may remain stale on other edges for up to the short public TTL. Admin mutations proactively evict in the handling location.\n- This release does not add Nginx caching to the Tailscale Bridge and does not migrate data away from Cloudflare D1/R2.\n- The first on-demand v14 migration is a one-time write operation. Avoid simultaneous manual D1 schema edits while deploying.\n"},{"name":"V14-0-3-PEER-SYNC-RECENT-LISTENED.md","content":"# WASP v14.0.3 — Peer metadata sync and Recently listened\n\n## Main changes\n\n**Admin → Network → Connected peers** now offers two actions on each peer:\n\n- **Refresh:** verifies the peer and then performs a fresh metadata sync (tracks, server playlists, and public user playlists). Unlike the old status-only refresh, this actively reloads the catalogue and publishes a local revision. The app's other open tabs receive a refresh signal and update automatically.\n- **☁ ↻ Cloud sync:** saves a new copy of the same peer metadata directly to the receiving WASP installation's D1 database, without downloading music files or image binaries. The sync interface shows actual completed stages (40%, 70%, 95%, 100%) above its status notification.\n\nThe source peer remains authoritative for playback. A snapshot stores track metadata, source identifiers, public playlist definitions, and artwork URLs, not the audio. If a snapshot becomes stale compared to a peer catalogue revision, the browser can fetch the newer live catalogue through the existing Docker peer relay.\n\n**Recently listened** is a new left-navigation page after Most listened. It displays up to 50 latest confirmed song plays with title, artist, cover, time, and play count; newest first. It combines this WASP installation's own history with read-only anonymous activity published by reachable, connected WASP peers. Updates are polled about every ten seconds; an edge cache can add up to roughly ten seconds of staleness. No listener names, IP addresses, or session identifiers are shared through the endpoint.\n\n**Admin → Network → Crons → Check connected peers for new tracks** includes a new *Sync peer catalogues and playlists* checkbox. It uses the existing enabled frequency/timezone schedule. All cron jobs remain disabled by default unless the administrator enables them.\n\nThe admin-only bottom-left version reads **WASP v14.0.3 · Peer Sync & Recently Listened**.\n\n## Non-destructive database upgrade\n\nThe schema marker advances from `14.2` to `14.3` and adds:\n\n- `peer_catalogue_cache` and `peer_catalogue_chunks` (peer metadata snapshots)\n- `recent_listens` and `idx_recent_listens_time` (anonymous recent play activity)\n\nD1 has a two-million-byte maximum row/string size. Snapshot records are chunked to approximately 240 KB each, well below that limit. A sync updates only the receiving server's metadata tables. It never modifies the source peer's music files or deletes user/admin playlist tables.\n\nPreviously played songs cannot be recovered into the new activity feed because historical individual play timestamps were not recorded. The feed starts accumulating after v14.0.3 deployment.\n\n## Cross-server limitations\n\n1. Both a receiving server and any source whose recent activity should be displayed must run WASP v14.0.3 or newer, since older Workers do not provide `/api/recent-listens`.\n2. The Docker server can read Cloudflare recent activity through its existing Nginx/Tailscale Bridge and same-origin relay. The public Cloudflare Worker cannot automatically reach a private-only Docker server, so Docker-only listening events cannot appear in Cloudflare's feed without an additional, secure and reachable publishing path.\n3. A stale metadata snapshot can keep a peer's cards visible while it is temporarily offline; **audio playback still requires a reachable source peer**. Syncing metadata does not host the audio locally.\n4. Rapid repeated manual syncs use D1 writes. Choose a conservative cron frequency, typically daily, and monitor D1 rows written as well as rows read.\n5. For very large or unusually verbose catalogues, syncing is bounded to an 8 MB response per collection and at most 6,000 tracks per peer in this release. Exceeding the limit returns an error; it does not delete source music.\n\n## Suggested staging rollout\n\n1. Back up both deployments' D1 databases and preserve R2 buckets, volumes, and Portainer mappings.\n2. Deploy the ZIP to a staging Cloudflare Worker with separate test resources. Then deploy the updated Docker source via a test Portainer stack.\n3. Add the public Cloudflare domain through the existing Bridge peer and click **☁ ↻** or **Refresh**. Confirm progress reaches 100%, that new tracks, new artists, and both types of public playlists appear, and that playback still uses the original peer.\n4. Play a song through Cloudflare for at least ten seconds, open **Recently listened** on Docker, and confirm it appears (allow a brief polling/cache delay).\n5. Enable the sync checkbox under the peers cron only after manual sync has been verified.\n6. Monitor Cloudflare D1 query and rows-written metrics to ensure periodic jobs are not causing excessive usage.\n\nNo new Docker volume mount, Tailscale exit node, or Nginx Bridge configuration is required.\n\n## Validation performed locally\n\n- JavaScript syntax checks\n- Node API regression suite, including admin authorization, D1 migration, metadata chunking for 1,400 tracks, and anonymous recent listening\n- Playwright browser checks for progress, scheduled sync checkbox, snapshot rendering, new peer revisions, and peer activity\n- ZIP integrity check\n\n**Not verified:** a complete Docker image build, a live Cloudflare D1 migration, or streaming between your production Docker, Bridge and Cloudflare servers. This is a staging candidate, not a production-verified release.\n"},{"name":"V14-0-2-LIBRARY-REFRESH-CRONS.md","content":"# WASP 14.0.2 — Catalogue freshness, listening stats, scheduled checks\n\nThis is an **additive staging release**, built on WASP v14.0.1 (the D1 SQL parameter-limit hotfix). It is not a replacement database, and it is not a new music library.\n\n## 1. New songs, new artists, and granted playlists\n\n- Successful changes to the local catalogue, administrator playlists, or public user playlists advance one D1 `settings.catalog_revision` value. Grants of playlist requests also advance the revision.\n- Browsers that remain open check this inexpensive version value approximately every 30 seconds while visible, instead of fetching the entire track catalogue every time.\n- On a revision change, the browser reloads tracks and playlists using a revision-specific cache key. Public Cloudflare edge caches can still reduce D1 reads between changes, while updated records are immediately available on the new cache key.\n- A Docker peer also exposes the revision over its same-origin relay; the browser checks the connected peers and refreshes when their catalogues change.\n- Uploaded artists are added to the existing normalized artist index without a full rebuild. After successful uploads, background tasks try the existing online biography, artist portrait, and album cover lookups. Missing or unavailable third-party information does not block uploads. The result is cached using existing D1/R2 records.\n- **Content freshness is normally within the next ~30-second visible-page check; it is not a real-time push.** If a tab is hidden or a peer is offline, it may refresh later.\n\n## 2. Most listened — local and connected peers\n\n- Docker now records a listening event for a peer-hosted track after **10 seconds of actual playback**, as it already does for local music.\n- The local Docker Worker stores these counts in a small new `peer_listens` table; it never deletes or edits original peer audio.\n- A public `/api/peer-plays` GET publishes **unsynced** counts only when network sharing is enabled. The browser aggregates source-host play counts and reachable connected-peer summaries.\n- With `PEER_PLAY_SYNC=1` (Docker default), Docker additionally attempts to POST each completed peer listen to the original peer's existing `/api/play/<track-id>` endpoint through the configured Bridge. A successful source acknowledgment is recorded in `synced_plays`; this event will then be excluded from `/api/peer-plays` so it is not counted twice. If the network POST fails, the event remains counted locally.\n- Set `PEER_RELAY_ALIASES` to a JSON map of public peer origin to local Bridge origin when using a Tailscale proxy, e.g. `{\"https://example-worker.example\":\"http://100.100.100.100:8081\"}`. The actual origin of a stored peer should still be the Bridge URL.\n- **Limit:** Docker-only tracks cannot automatically appear in Cloudflare's Most listened if the Cloudflare Worker cannot reach the Docker server and Docker does not securely publish that track metadata. For music originally hosted on Cloudflare, Docker's successful play reports update the source-host play counter, so those plays appear in Cloudflare rankings. Wider cross-origin aggregation requires a separate authenticated outbound publishing design or an externally reachable trusted HTTPS peer.\n\n## 3. Admin → Network → Crons\n\nOpen **Admin → Network → Crons**. This tab matches the existing WASP admin theme and is visible only to administrators.\n\nEach checkbox is disabled by default:\n\n1. Check connected peers for new tracks (compares the number of tracks advertised by each reachable peer and updates its status/track count).\n2. Fetch missing artist and album artwork.\n3. Fetch artist biographies and lineups.\n\nConfigure the timezone once (IANA timezone, e.g. `Asia/Manila`). Every job can run:\n\n- **Daily** — at a chosen 24-hour `HH:MM` local time.\n- **Weekly** — select Sunday through Saturday; executes at **00:00** local time on that day.\n- **Monthly** — select day **1 through 30**; executes at **00:00** local time on that day. Months lacking the selected day skip it.\n\nCloudflare executes a UTC scheduled Worker trigger once a minute, and WASP interprets the selected timezone inside the handler. Docker uses a local one-minute scheduler and authenticates it with its persistent scanner secret. Each job is guarded against running repeatedly within the same scheduled minute. Artwork/biography jobs process a bounded batch of up to eight newer tracks per tick, not the whole library.\n\n**Caution:** Scheduling more frequent checks increases API calls, Cloudflare Worker invocations and potentially D1 queries. Keep schedules disabled until the deployment is stable; then start with one daily schedule, observe metrics, and enable others as needed.\n\nThe Cron check only refreshes peer status and catalogue metadata. It does not download, move, or delete original music files.\n\n## 4. Admin-only version indicator\n\nThe bottom-left of `/admin` and all admin hash pages reads the running application's version from `/api/admin/version` (administrator authentication required):\n\n`WASP v14.0.2 · Library Refresh & Peer Listening`\n\nThe main listener website does not show the build version. Checking the badge after a Portainer rebuild can help distinguish stale images from a deployed source revision.\n\n## 5. Data preservation, deployment and rollback\n\n- **Back up production D1** (Cloudflare Time Travel or a verified SQL export) and note the last working Worker deployment before upgrading.\n- Deploy to a separate staging Worker with separate D1 and R2 resources where possible. Do not point staging at the live R2 bucket or D1 database.\n- Upgrade v14.0.1 to v14.0.2 adds the `peer_listens` table and an index on `tracks.created_at` and changes a schema marker. It does not truncate `tracks`, `playlists`, `playlist_tracks`, `user_playlists`, `reactions`, `artist_info`, or other existing records. It does not delete or move R2 objects.\n- Do **not** restore D1 to an earlier time just to roll back the Worker. Roll back the Worker deployment code if needed. Additional v14.0.2 tables are additive and can remain while using the previous Worker.\n- When updating Docker in Portainer, **retain** existing volumes, upload data, state and music-folder bindings. Do not use `docker volume prune` or delete the stack's volumes.\n- The source ZIP does not include `.env` to avoid overwriting site-specific credentials or host volume paths. Configure production env vars in Portainer / Cloudflare Secrets as appropriate.\n- Cloudflare Cron Triggers are declared in `wrangler.jsonc`. They may take time to propagate. All three application jobs start **disabled** even though the minute trigger is configured.\n- Docker image/build tests and live Cloudflare/Tailscale playback were **not** performed in the development environment. These remain staging/production acceptance checks.\n\n## 6. Acceptance checks\n\n1. Upload a single track that has a new artist. Confirm its song and artist appear in a fresh `/#recent` and Artists view; then leave a second tab open, and verify it reloads within a polling interval while visible.\n2. Grant a playlist request or create an admin playlist. Verify it appears in `/#playlists` without clearing browser caches or resetting the Worker.\n3. Check online biography and cover lookup for the newly uploaded artist; external sources may have no data.\n4. Play a peer-origin track through Docker for more than ten seconds. Verify the local Most listened chart; confirm the source Cloudflare `plays` value also grows when `PEER_PLAY_SYNC=1` and the source `/api/play` POST is reachable through Bridge.\n5. Configure a safe test cron schedule. Confirm saving, timezone handling and one run per selected minute. Turn it back off after verification.\n6. Check `Admin → Network` still displays connected peers, `/admin` displays the current version, and no existing administrator or user playlists have disappeared.\n7. Monitor D1 rows read/written for 30–60 minutes after upload completion and after active browsing; do not compare an upload-heavy interval with a quiet interval without accounting for traffic.\n\n## Test coverage\n\nThe packaged project includes the existing Worker, Docker gateway, browser, and reaction tests, plus `tests/v14_0_2_refresh_crons_peerplays.test.mjs` and `tests/ui_v14_0_2_refresh_crons.py`. They cover catalogue revision invalidation, new playlist visibility, local and synced peer-play counting, cron access controls, weekly/monthly local-time triggering and duplicate suppression, authenticated admin version UI, and the additive upgrade from schema version 14.\n"},{"name":"V14-0-1-D1-HOTFIX.md","content":"# WASP v14.0.1 — Emergency Cloudflare D1 hotfix\n\n## Incident and cause\n\nThe original v14 backfill used 35 mapping rows per `INSERT`, with three bound parameters per mapping: **105 bound parameters**, exceeding Cloudflare D1's **100-bound-parameter** limit. On a library with enough tracks, the initial migration fails with `D1_ERROR: too many SQL variables`, returning HTTP 500 for the Worker and preventing peer discovery. This was missed in the old small-fixture tests.\n\n## Exact correction\n\n`src/index.js`: batch `track_artist_map` backfill in groups of **30 rows / 90 bound parameters**. An additional regression test `tests/v14_d1_parameter_limit.test.mjs` simulates the Cloudflare bound-parameter limit while migrating 155 tracks (310 artist mappings), so the original failure is detected.\n\n## Production recovery\n\n1. **To restore availability immediately**, roll back only the Cloudflare Worker application to the last functioning v13 deployment using Workers & Pages -> your Worker -> Deployments -> prior stable version -> Rollback. This does not roll back D1 or R2 data. v13 ignores the extra v14 artist lookup table.\n2. Confirm `GET /api/peer` and `GET /api/tracks` work on `music.playy.online`, and test a playlist. Do not delete tables, restore an old database snapshot, or reset R2.\n3. Back up D1, deploy v14.0.1 to staging with a separate test D1 database, then stage with a production-like number of tracks. Test the full library, artist pages, playlists, reactions, and peer relay.\n4. Only after staging passes, deploy v14.0.1 to production. The failed v14 backfill should complete on a subsequent request once any expired migration lease is released. No manual D1 cleanup is necessary. If a failed migration left a lease, wait up to 90 seconds for it to expire and retry.\n5. Confirm `SELECT value FROM settings WHERE key = 'wasp_schema_version'` returns `14` and `/api/peer` returns HTTP 200. Watch D1 rows read and written after deployment.\n\n## Separate Docker incident\n\nA simultaneous Portainer rebuild failed with `no space left on device` during Docker image extraction. This is a Docker-host disk-space issue, **not** the D1 API failure. Check `df -h`, `sudo docker system df`, and `/var/lib/docker` capacity. You can usually reclaim unused build cache with `sudo docker builder prune` or expand the EC2 EBS filesystem. **Do not prune volumes or remove running containers**; preserve the persistent WASP database, music directories, and Portainer data. Fix Cloudflare availability first, then revisit Docker disk capacity.\n\n## Validation\n\n- Node/SQLite regression: 155 tracks, 310 artist mappings, max 90 bound parameters — pass.\n- Original v14 query/caching test — pass.\n- Existing JavaScript Worker API and gateway tests — pass.\n- **Not yet tested:** production D1 execution or complete Docker image build.\n"},{"name":"V13-WASP-UPDATE.md","content":"# WASP v13 — Branding and administration update\n\nThis package extends the working RiffVault v13 peer-relay project; it does not automatically deploy to any Cloudflare Worker. Preserve production D1/R2 bindings, and test separately before enabling deployment workflows.\n\n- `Admin → General settings → Branding`: choose the site name and whether to show its uploaded browser icon before the top-left name. Default WASP icon bundled in `public/wasp-icon.png`; upload a browser icon to override it.\n- Username registration matches the existing password input style.\n- Player and album `+` buttons present an authentication modal for guests with Register and Log in choices.\n- Artist biography, lineup and similar-artist data can be retrieved through an approved peer in Docker relay mode when the artist is not hosted locally.\n- `Admin → Users`: read-only registered user counts. Historical browser-anonymous reactions cannot be retroactively assigned to accounts; only future authenticated song reactions contribute to per-account counts.\n- Request form action spacing improved.\n\n**Security:** Any old default administrator password must be changed before exposing the application. Do not make your music library publicly shareable until you have set a unique strong password.\n\n## Deployment safety\n\n- Publish changes to a test branch/Portainer stack first; production Cloudflare is not updated by this archive.\n- The existing Docker service name, image name, Worker name and D1/R2 bindings are unchanged intentionally so data volumes and Cloudflare resources are not replaced.\n- Portainer Git stacks must rebuild the Docker image (not merely restart the old one) to include updated HTML, relay allowlist and Worker code.\n- The new `user_song_reactions` table is additive; no existing music files, reactions or playlists are deleted.\n- Browser icon visibility is opt-in under Admin → General settings.\n"},{"name":"V13-WASP-PEER-REACTIONS-SPOTIFY.md","content":"# WASP v13 — branding, local peer reactions, and playlist search\n\nThis project continues the WASP v13 Docker peer-relay build. The change is additive and does not require resetting the existing database or replacing music files.\n\n## What is included\n\n- `README.md` now opens with WASP branding and a **transparent-background** wasp mascot (`public/wasp-mascot.png`).\n- Logged-out visitors see **Sign In** next to the account icon. Account names appear after authentication.\n- Tracks streamed from registered peers support **Heart**, **Like**, and **Dislike**. Votes are stored on the listening WASP server in its own D1 database; they are **not** sent to the remote music host. The backend validates peer registration. Likes and dislikes are mutually exclusive. Authenticated user votes contribute to the local Users statistics going forward; earlier anonymous votes cannot be retroactively attributed.\n- Artist artwork falls back to other songs by that artist when the online artist image cannot load. When a Docker host has MP3s with embedded artwork, the existing `/art/track` handler supplies it during the fallback chain. Source music remains unchanged.\n- Under **Request → Make a request → Playlist**, hosts with Spotify API credentials can search Spotify playlist metadata and select a result, which fills the original Spotify playlist link field. Copy/paste links work without search credentials.\n\n## Spotify developer credentials\n\nCreate an app at https://developer.spotify.com/dashboard and obtain its Client ID and Client Secret. Spotify developer policies and account limitations apply. Some applications may not have access to the Spotify Search endpoint, and rate limits can apply. WASP shows an error and leaves the paste-link option available in that case.\n\n**Docker / Portainer:** Set `SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID` and `SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET` under the stack's environment variables, then recreate the container. The Docker start script writes a temporary, container-local `.dev.vars` file readable by Wrangler; it does not put the secret into an HTTP endpoint or browser bundle. Protect the Portainer admin interface and the server.\n\n**Local developer:** Create a gitignored `.dev.vars` beside `wrangler.jsonc` containing:\n\n```dotenv\nSPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=\"YOUR_CLIENT_ID\"\nSPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=\"YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET\"\n```\n\n**Cloudflare Worker:** Add `SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID` and `SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET` as Worker secrets using Cloudflare's dashboard or `npx wrangler secret put`. Do not commit secrets in Wrangler config or Git. Deploy to a staging Worker first, then explicitly promote your tested version.\n\nSpotify playlist search retrieves only playlist metadata. It does not copy or download Spotify audio; playlist requests continue through WASP's existing queue.\n\n## Tests\n\n```bash\nnode tests/wasp_v13_peer_spotify.test.mjs\npython tests/ui_wasp_v13_extra.py\n```\n\nThe existing Worker, Docker gateway, peer relay, and browser regression tests remain in `tests/`.\n\n## Deployment safety\n\nBack up existing Docker data volumes before redeploying the stack. Do not run `docker compose down -v` and do not share the default administrator password. No Cloudflare production data or running EC2 instances were changed by producing this source package.\n"},{"name":"V13-SPOTIFY-ADMIN-SETTINGS.md","content":"# WASP v13 — Spotify settings in Admin → Requests\n\n## Administrator workflow\n\n1. Sign in as administrator and open **Admin → Requests → Spotify settings** (`/admin.html#requests/spotify`).\n2. Paste the Client ID and Client Secret from your Spotify Developer application.\n3. Select **Save credentials**. The Client Secret is never displayed again or included in subsequent API responses.\n4. Use **Request → Make a request → Playlist → Search** to look up public Spotify playlists. The existing paste-link field remains available.\n5. To change the Client ID but keep the existing encrypted secret, leave the secret input blank. To rotate it, enter a new secret.\n6. **Remove saved credentials** removes only the WASP database copy; it does not revoke credentials on Spotify or remove any environment-supplied values.\n\n## Encryption and deployment\n\n- Only authenticated administrators can call `GET`, `PUT`, and `DELETE /api/admin/spotify-config`.\n- Saved credentials use AES-GCM and a random IV. The encryption key comes from `SPOTIFY_SETTINGS_KEY` or Docker's persisted `SCANNER_SECRET`; it is **not** stored with the encrypted credentials.\n- Docker creates its installation secret automatically and persists it in the application-state volume. Back up the volume. Avoid changing `SCANNER_SECRET` afterward.\n- For Cloudflare Workers, provision a persistent random `SPOTIFY_SETTINGS_KEY` as a Worker secret **once** before using the new admin form. If it is missing, saving is refused with an explanation. The Worker environment-credential fallback remains supported.\n- Database-saved settings take precedence over environment-supplied Spotify credentials; deleting the saved settings restores the environment fallback.\n- If the encryption key changes, saved credentials cannot be decrypted. Restoring the previous key or entering new credentials with the current key resolves that condition.\n- Use HTTPS when entering credentials, and never share your Spotify secret or encryption key.\n- Spotify API access and search availability are ultimately subject to Spotify's permissions and policies. An API credential pair is not a guarantee of playlist search access.\n\n## Validation\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\nnode --experimental-sqlite tests/spotify_admin_config.test.mjs\npython tests/ui_spotify_admin.py\n```\n\nThese exercise admin authentication, encrypted storage, masked secrets, key rotation failure, token-cache invalidation, environment fallback, Requests subtab styling, navigation and save/remove actions.\n\nThis update has not been deployed to the user's live Cloudflare Worker or Docker host.\n"},{"name":"V13-SIMILAR-PEER-REACTIONS.md","content":"# WASP v13 — Similar Artists and Local Peer Reactions\n\nThis package continues the v13 Docker peer-relay and Spotify administration release. It does **not** require changing your Tailscale settings or Cloudflare Worker just to use the fixes on a Docker host.\n\n## Similar Artists\n\nBoth the Similar Artists tab and the full `/#similar/<artist>` page now request recommendations through the same-origin relay registered for each connected peer. An empty response from one source no longer prevents another eligible peer from returning recommendations. These are actual responses supplied by a configured server's recommendation endpoint; when none return results, the UI still correctly reports that no similar artists were found.\n\nThe artist cards use precise availability labels:\n\n- **In your library**: this WASP host contains a track by the artist.\n- **Peer Library**: a connected peer contains a track by the artist, but this host does not. Clicking opens that artist's page and peer-hosted songs.\n- **Not in the library**: no song by that artist is found in the currently loaded local or connected-peer catalogue; clicking offers to request the artist.\n\nIf a peer's similar-artist provider cannot find recommendations (or its API is unavailable), WASP cannot manufacture results. The old local-only restriction in the full-page lookup has been corrected.\n\n## Reactions\n\n- Heart, Like and Dislike buttons remain available to guests and signed-in listeners for both local and peer tracks.\n- A click updates the visible count immediately (optimistically). The local Worker persists votes and returns the authoritative count; failures show an error and undo the optimistic update.\n- When a song is playing, the player periodically fetches only its reaction counts, including the current visitor's vote, so other listeners' votes become visible without refreshing the page.\n- Every peer song is identified by the configured peer origin **and** original track ID. The source peer's database and actual music files are never changed when a local user votes.\n- Visitor identity is preserved in the browser's local storage; signed-in user-linked reaction statistics are still recorded by the existing server logic. Anonymous browser data is not automatically merged into an account.\n\n## Deployment\n\n1. Back up the existing Docker data volumes.\n2. Update the **staging** GitHub repository from the full ZIP and have Portainer rebuild the image when redeploying.\n3. Keep any existing peer URL and `PEER_RELAY_ALIASES` settings in Portainer unchanged.\n4. Verify `/#similar/<peer artist>` on your Docker WASP instance and check that a **Peer Library** artist link opens its music.\n5. Play a peer track, click Heart, and confirm the count increments immediately. Open a second browser to check that it receives the saved count after its next refresh.\n\nDo not delete Docker volumes or overwrite production Cloudflare data. No new environment variables or destructive database migrations are required for these changes.\n\n## Automated checks\n\n- `node tests/v13_similar_reactions.test.mjs`\n- `python tests/ui_v13_similar_reactions.py`\n- Existing `tests/*.test.mjs`, `tests/api_smoke.mjs`, and `tests/ui*.py`\n\nA live Docker build and a live network-to-Cloudflare smoke test are still needed before calling this a stable production release.\n"},{"name":"V13-PLAYLIST-ARTIST-RATINGS-FIX.md","content":"# WASP v13 — Artist and playlist reaction rankings\n\nThis update is based on `WASP-v13-peer-reaction-charts-fix.zip`. It preserves the existing Docker, Cloudflare Worker, D1, R2, and peer-relay deployment configuration.\n\n## Artist rankings\n\nFixes the `libNames is not defined` exception on **Most hearted / Most liked / Most disliked → Artists**. Lists are ranked from reactions saved on the current WASP installation, including artists available only through approved connected peers. A changed artist vote does not modify the peer's original database.\n\n## Playlist ratings\n\nPlaylist detail pages now display **♡ Heart** (with its text label), an icon-only **Like** button, and an icon-only **Dislike** button. They work on the local server's playlists, public user playlists, and connected peers' shared playlists. Guests and signed-in users can vote with their existing per-visitor ID. Like and Dislike are mutually exclusive; Heart is independent. Counts update optimistically and the interface rolls back an unsuccessful save.\n\nThe playlist ranking pages combine server playlists, shared peer playlists, and public user playlists and use **this server's votes** for all three rankings. Peer playlist keys are scoped by source origin and remote playlist ID so an ID collision between servers does not combine votes. Minimal metadata for voted peer playlists is saved locally so their ranking entries remain recognizable while the peer is temporarily offline. The audio files are not copied, and playback still depends on the peer being reachable.\n\nExisting hearts saved through `/api/playlist-heart` are copied into the unified ranking table using an idempotent, non-destructive migration. The original heart table remains intact. A new `voted_playlist_cache` table holds peer playlist display metadata only. No music objects, tracks, existing playlists, or user accounts are deleted.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe bundled automated tests cover the artist ranking page, peer/local/public playlist voting, all three playlist ranking pages, unauthorized or unknown peer inputs, old hearts migration, peer source metadata, and the existing WASP regression suite.\n\nDeploy into staging first and rebuild the Docker image. Back up the persistent Docker state before updating. Do not use `docker compose down -v`. This package does not change the already-running Cloudflare production service or your Tailscale bridge. An end-to-end test on your live EC2 installation has **not** been performed.\n"},{"name":"V13-PEER-RELAY.md","content":"# RiffVault v13 — Docker peer relay\n\nRiffVault v13 keeps the v12 music library, accounts and playlists, and adds a **Docker-only same-origin relay** for a connected peer whose HTTP address is private or blocked from visitors' browsers.\n\n## How this works\n\nBrowser -> RiffVault Docker (same HTTP(S) origin) -> Node gateway -> connected peer URL (e.g. Tailscale Bridge/Nginx) -> Cloudflare/RiffVault source.\n\nOnly the Docker instance contacts the Tailscale address. A browser no longer fetches the peer catalogue, playlists, public-user playlist covers, or audio from a `100.x.x.x` URL. Audio remains on the source peer and is **streamed on demand** with HTTP byte-range support; it is not copied into the local music library.\n\nThe server only relays **read-only**, explicitly allowlisted public peer routes (`/api/tracks`, `/api/playlists`, `/api/user-playlists`, `/api/peer`, `/api/site`, `/art/...` and `/audio/<id>`). Only administrators can register remote peer servers. The relay strips session cookies/credentials and rejects off-site redirects; it is not a general-purpose network proxy.\n\n## Deploy from the working v12 installation through Portainer\n\n1. **Back up** the Docker data volume, user playlists, and current Git revision. Keep the existing v12 deployment available for rollback.\n2. Extract this ZIP into your **staging Git branch/repository** (the repository selected by Portainer). It has the original `docker-compose.yml` and all v12 sources with targeted v13 changes. Do not push to a Git branch that automatically redeploys the production Cloudflare Worker unless that is intentional.\n3. Commit and push the update. Under **Portainer -> Stacks -> riffvault**, use **Pull and redeploy**, rebuilding the Docker image. The v13 Compose file sets `pull_policy: build` to request a source rebuild. Confirm that the newly built image was deployed rather than merely restarting the old container.\n4. Keep the existing Portainer environment variables and Docker volumes. **Do not remove volumes or run `docker compose down -v`.** No database reset or destructive migration is required.\n5. In **Admin -> Connected peers**, add the Tailscale-accessible bridge URL, e.g. `http://100.107.221.71:8081`. The peer's `/api/peer` endpoint should report the shared music library, and the Bridge Nginx service must reach the real music host.\n6. Open RiffVault from the private-network browser; go to **Playlists -> Peer playlists** or the main music catalogue. Browser DevTools should show requests to `/_peer/<peer-id>/api/tracks` and `/_peer/<peer-id>/audio/<track-id>`, **not** direct requests to `100.107.221.71:8081`.\n\n> The most reliable production setup uses a trusted HTTPS domain for Docker RiffVault. Plain HTTP over the public internet exposes login information. Restrict the EC2/security group until the default administrator password has been changed.\n\n## Optional: canonical-origin alias for third-party playlist references\n\nIf a playlist on the bridge reports songs as hosted by the *original* Cloudflare URL (`https://music.playy.online`) rather than its *bridge* URL, configure the optional Portainer stack environment variable:\n\n```text\nPEER_RELAY_ALIASES={\"https://music.playy.online\":\"http://100.107.221.71:8081\"}\n```\n\nThis is a JSON object mapping **canonical public origins** to **already connected, admin-approved peer URLs**. It affects only how the browser selects the local relay for existing source references. It does not let clients choose arbitrary upstream websites. Redeploy the Docker container after changing it.\n\nThe alias is not needed for a normal playlist whose tracks are all directly hosted by the connected bridge. For songs hosted by unrelated third-party servers, add those source servers as approved peers rather than assuming they live behind this bridge.\n\n## Verify after the update\n\nOn the Docker EC2 host (container name `riffvault`):\n\n```bash\n# Must return 200 and a nonzero byte count\ncurl -sS -o /dev/null -w 'HTTP %{http_code}, %{size_download} bytes\\n' http://127.0.0.1:8080/\n\n# Confirm the registry includes the relay_base fields\ncurl -sS http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/network/peers\n\n# Verify the local gateway is running\nsudo docker exec riffvault node -e 'fetch(\"http://127.0.0.1:8788/api/network/peers\").then(r=>r.text()).then(console.log).catch(console.error)'\n```\n\nTo test media playback in a browser, select a peer-hosted song, play it and seek. The Network panel should show an audio request on your **Docker RiffVault hostname**, with HTTP `200` or `206 Partial Content` depending on the request.\n\n## Known limitations and safety\n\n- **Cloudflare Workers production is not modified.** Docker handles the relay and keeps its D1/R2-emulated storage independent from Cloudflare production.\n- The source host must expose working `/audio` and `/art` routes. URLs to unrelated external image/CDN hosts are not automatically proxied; the existing nine-tile artwork fallback remains available.\n- If a peer goes offline, the relay returns a gateway error; it does not automatically download or permanently cache that peer's songs.\n- Relayed audio uses Docker/EC2 bandwidth and may incur AWS transfer costs. Access to a public RiffVault server can use the relay; apply appropriate server/network limits.\n- For browsers in restricted private networks, Tailscale itself does not need to be installed on the client. Do **not** change your Docker EC2's default route to a Tailscale exit node.\n- `curl /api/network/peers` succeeds even when the peer is offline because the registry is local. A working remote track request must be checked separately.\n\n## Run included isolated checks\n\n```bash\nnode --test tests/peer_relay.test.mjs tests/gateway_proxy.test.mjs\npython tests/ui_peer_relay_smoke.py\npython tests/ui_smoke.py\nnode tests/api_smoke.mjs\n```\n\nBrowser tests require Python Playwright with Chromium. The tests use local mock peers and do not touch actual Cloudflare music, accounts or databases.\n"},{"name":"V13-PEER-REACTION-CHARTS.md","content":"# WASP v13 — Peer reactions in popularity charts\n\nThis update makes **Most hearted**, **Most liked**, and **Most disliked** include peer-hosted songs that receive votes on the current WASP instance.\n\n- Counts on a peer song represent votes stored **on this WASP server**, not votes submitted to the source peer.\n- Guest and signed-in user votes continue to work without uploading or deleting any remote audio.\n- Popularity charts combine matching recordings into one card when the local server and a connected peer have the same song.\n- The local server stores a small amount of track metadata alongside peer reactions, allowing a previously reacted-to song to remain visible in rankings while its peer catalogue is temporarily unavailable. Audio playback still requires a reachable source server.\n- Votes in the music player update immediately. Chart pages also refresh vote totals automatically while visible.\n- Existing database tables are preserved. Five optional columns are added to the existing `peer_reaction_tracks` table through additive migrations.\n- The browser still uses the configured same-origin peer relay. No Tailscale exit-node or Cloudflare production changes are required.\n\n## Deployment and testing\n\nUpdate your staging Git repository and **rebuild** the Docker image using Portainer. Keep existing Docker volumes. Do not use `docker compose down -v`.\n\nVerify by playing a song supplied by a connected peer, clicking Heart/Like/Dislike, and visiting `/#hearts`, `/#likes` or `/#dislikes`. The song should appear with the local vote count. You can repeat this in a private browsing window to verify guest voting. If the peer is temporarily unavailable, existing metadata and votes remain visible, but audio may not play.\n\nAutomated checks include `node tests/v13_peer_reaction_charts.test.mjs` and `python tests/ui_peer_reaction_charts.py`.\n\nThis is a staging build; it has not been deployed to the user's live EC2 or Cloudflare environment.\n"}];
